<section #sh-config | `<config>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *config*. Compiles to eHTML `<config>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<config>`

<code #code:sh-config-1>
<config citation-style=author-year number-sections=true />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-config-1>
<config citation-style=author-year number-sections=true />
</minipage>

*The authoring form for &lt;config&gt; is kwargs. Settings are read at the
discovery pass into the configuration registry; the element itself
produces no body output (it renders as an empty &lt;config&gt;).*

<code #code:sh-config-2>
<config number-figures=true number-tables=true show-source=true />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-config-2>
<config number-figures=true number-tables=true show-source=true />
</minipage>

*More operational options, all from the live kwarg allowlist
(citation-style, number-sections, number-figures, number-tables,
number-equations, show-source, parse-data-tables, ref-prefix-*).*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `notes` | kwarg |  |  |

<section #sh-date | `<date>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *date*. Compiles to eHTML `<date>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<date>`

<code #code:sh-date-1>
<date | 2024-03-15>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-date-1>
<date | 2024-03-15>
</minipage>

*A bare &lt;date&gt; is UNTYPED (no data-date-type) — an authoring date is
"when you wrote it" (like the date on a letter), not a publication
date (cf. Quarto/Pandoc/Bookdown preamble date slots). Use an explicit
type= (e.g. &lt;date type=publication | …&gt;) for a typed date. (#325)*

<code #code:sh-date-2>
<date type=submission | 2023-11-01>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-date-2>
<date type=submission | 2023-11-01>
</minipage>

<code-block #code:sh-date-3>
<meta>
  <date type=submission | 2023-11-01>
  <date type=acceptance | 2024-02-10>
  <date type=publication | 2024-03-15>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-date-3>
<meta>
  <date type=submission | 2023-11-01>
  <date type=acceptance | 2024-02-10>
  <date type=publication | 2024-03-15>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Multiple dates of different types. The publication date is the
primary; submission, acceptance, etc., go in JATS history.*

<code #code:sh-date-4>
<date | March 15, 2024>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-date-4>
<date | March 15, 2024>
</minipage>

*Free-form date format. Acceptable but ISO 8601 is preferred for
machine readability and for predictable JATS export.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `publication` `submission` `acceptance` `revision` `retraction` `embargo` `other` (default `publication`) | Distinguishes different kinds of dates. |
| `format` | kwarg | `iso` `ymd` `ymd-time` `mdy` `dmy` `custom` | Optional hint about how the date should be parsed and formatted. |

<section #sh-editor | `<editor>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *editor*. Compiles to eHTML `<editor>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<editor>`

<code #code:sh-editor-1>
<editor | The Editor>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-editor-1>
<editor | The Editor>
</minipage>

*Casual form — the pipe content is the editor's name (text content,
not lifted to a &lt;name&gt; child), mirroring &lt;author | ...&gt;.*

<code #code:sh-editor-2>
<editor role=series-editor name="Jane Goodall" affiliation="Cambridge University" />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-editor-2>
<editor role=series-editor name="Jane Goodall" affiliation="Cambridge University" />
</minipage>

*Kwarg form. name / affiliation / orcid / email lift to child tags at
the gate (unified to &lt;author&gt;'s shape, #338); role stays a scalar
attribute (data-role) — the one field that differs from &lt;author&gt;.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `name` | kwarg |  | The editor's name. |
| `affiliation` | kwarg |  | Editor's institutional affiliation. |
| `orcid` | kwarg |  | Editor's ORCID identifier. |
| `email` | kwarg |  | Editor's contact email address. |
| `role` | kwarg | `editor` `co-editor` `series-editor` `volume-editor` `guest-editor` `other` (default `editor`) | The editorial role — the one field that distinguishes an &lt;editor&gt; from an &lt;author&gt;. |

<section #sh-email | `<email>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *email*. Compiles to eHTML `<email>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<email>`

<code-block #code:sh-email-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <email | jane@example.org>
</author>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-email-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <email | jane@example.org>
</author>
</minipage>

*Author contact email. Common in journal article metadata for
the corresponding author (see also the +corresponding boolean
kwarg on &lt;author&gt;).*

<section #sh-keywords | `<keywords>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *keywords*. Compiles to eHTML `<keywords>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<keywords>`

<code-block #code:sh-keywords-1>
<meta>
  <keywords | elephants, conservation, climate, carbon storage>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-keywords-1>
<meta>
  <keywords | elephants, conservation, climate, carbon storage>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Comma-separated keyword list — the simpler and more common
authoring form. The exporter splits on commas when emitting JATS.*

<code #code:sh-keywords-2>
<meta keywords="elephants, conservation, climate" />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-keywords-2>
<meta keywords="elephants, conservation, climate" />
</minipage>

*Kwarg-form authoring lifts to the child-tag form at the gate.*

<section #sh-lang | `<lang>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *lang*. Compiles to eHTML `<lang>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<lang>`

<code-block #code:sh-lang-1>
<meta>
  <lang | en-US>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-lang-1>
<meta>
  <lang | en-US>
</meta>
</minipage>

*BCP 47 language tag. The eHTML form preserves the value as a
child element of &lt;meta&gt;; downstream consumers (the JATS exporter,
the render-mode lowering) project it where each format expects.*

<code #code:sh-lang-2>
<meta lang="fr" />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-lang-2>
<meta lang="fr" />
</minipage>

*Kwarg-form authoring lifts to the child-tag form at the gate.*

<section #sh-license | `<license>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *license*. Compiles to eHTML `<license>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<license>`

<code-block #code:sh-license-1>
<meta>
  <license href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" | CC BY 4.0>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-license-1>
<meta>
  <license href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" | CC BY 4.0>
</meta>
</minipage>

*License name with canonical URL.*

<code #code:sh-license-2>
<meta license="MIT License" />
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-license-2>
<meta license="MIT License" />
</minipage>

*Kwarg-form authoring (license name only) lifts to the child-tag
form at the normalize-to-canonical gate.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `href` | kwarg |  | Optional URL of the license terms (e.g. |

<section #sh-meta | `<meta>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *meta*. Compiles to eHTML `<meta>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<meta>`

<code-block #code:sh-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-meta-1>
<meta type=article>
  <title | The Effect of Elephants on Climate>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
  <date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>

<section | Introduction>
The paper begins.
</minipage>

*Author writes &lt;meta type=article&gt; at the top with no &lt;article&gt;
wrapper. The structural plugin reads type=article and generates:
  - the &lt;article&gt; container
  - &lt;article-front&gt; wrapping the original &lt;meta&gt;
  - &lt;article-body&gt; wrapping the section content
&lt;title&gt; is promoted to &lt;article-title&gt; as the first child of &lt;meta&gt;.
&lt;meta&gt; itself survives in the output, inside &lt;article-front&gt;.*

<code-block #code:sh-meta-2>
<meta type=book>
  <title | A Natural History of Elephants>
  <author | Jane Goodall>
</meta>

<chapter | Origins>
Content.
</code-block>

*(Rendered preview omitted — a book (`<meta type=book>` / `<chapter>`) is a whole-document structure, which a sealed preview box cannot host.)*

*type=book generates the book-shaped wrapper instead. Changing the
single kwarg switches the entire output structure. Each book-part
contains its own &lt;meta&gt; with &lt;book-part-title&gt;; no &lt;book-part-meta&gt;
wrapper.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `article` `book` `book-part` `website` (default `article`) | Declares the document class. |
| `book-part-type` | kwarg | `chapter` `part` `introduction` `conclusion` `other` `preface` `foreword` `dedication` `appendix` `glossary` `colophon` `afterword` (default `chapter`) | For a single-file book-part (type=book-part) only: |
| `icon` | kwarg |  | Optional brand icon for a website's top bar (#246): |
| `slug` | kwarg |  | The page's stable public SLUG (#289) — its identity, independent of title and of where it sits in a website's &lt;nav&gt;. |

<section #sh-name | `<name>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *name*. Compiles to eHTML `<name>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<name>`

<code-block #code:sh-name-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
</author>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-name-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
</author>
</minipage>

*The common case. Pipe content becomes the name string.*

<code #code:sh-name-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" +corresponding>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-name-2>
<author name="Jane Goodall" orcid="0000-0001-2345-6789" +corresponding>
</minipage>

*Kwarg form of &lt;author&gt;. The `name` kwarg lifts to a &lt;name&gt; child
tag at the normalize-to-canonical gate, parallel to &lt;meta&gt;'s
kwarg-to-child-tag lift. The `+corresponding` boolean stays as
a kwarg/attribute on the canonical eHTML &lt;author&gt; (it is a
scalar marker, not a structured field).*

<section #sh-orcid | `<orcid>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *orcid*. Compiles to eHTML `<orcid>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<orcid>`

<code-block #code:sh-orcid-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <orcid | 0000-0002-1825-0097>
</author>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-orcid-1>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <orcid | 0000-0002-1825-0097>
</author>
</minipage>

*Bare 16-digit ORCID — the canonical form.*

<code-block #code:sh-orcid-2>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <orcid | https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1825-0097>
</author>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-orcid-2>
<author>
  <name | Jane Goodall>
  <orcid | https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1825-0097>
</author>
</minipage>

*URL form is accepted but bare form is preferred.*

<section #sh-publication-date | `<publication-date>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *publication-date*. Compiles to eHTML `<publication-date>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<publication-date>`

<code-block #code:sh-publication-date-1>
<meta>
  <publication-date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-publication-date-1>
<meta>
  <publication-date | 2024-03-15>
</meta>
</minipage>

*ISO 8601 publication date — the preferred form.*

<code-block #code:sh-publication-date-2>
<meta>
  <publication-date | March 15, 2024>
</meta>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-publication-date-2>
<meta>
  <publication-date | March 15, 2024>
</meta>
</minipage>

*Free-form publication date. Acceptable but ISO 8601 is preferred
for predictable JATS export and machine readability.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | kwarg | `iso` `ymd` `ymd-time` `mdy` `dmy` `custom` | Optional hint about how the date should be parsed and formatted. |

